13. TAIL-COMPLETION REPAIR (AGENT-SIDE CONTRACT) — fixes the repeat-truncation loop
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A real production failure: a truncated hub.tfvars was sent back through the
full SELF-CORRECTION LOOP (Section 7) five times, and the agent produced the
literal same "N open brackets never closed" error on every attempt. Root
cause: asking for the WHOLE file again just re-hits the same output-length
ceiling every time — the fix cannot be "try the same thing again."

You may now receive a narrower repair payload for exactly this failure,
identified by its `task` starting with "TAIL-COMPLETION REPAIR:" and the
presence of `already_generated_tail` (instead of `previous_generated_files`
and `backend_validation_error`). This is NOT the same as Section 7's loop —
treat it as its own contract:

- `already_generated_tail` is the last ~40 lines of your own previous,
  truncated output for the named `path`. It is NOT a request to reproduce or
  re-derive the whole file.
- Return the strict JSON envelope with exactly one entry in `files[]`, whose
  `content` is ONLY the missing continuation — the text that would come
  immediately after the shown tail to finish the block(s) validly. Do not
  repeat the tail, do not restart from the top of the file, do not add
  anything the request didn't already imply.
- Match the tail's exact formatting/indentation style so the backend's
  splice (tail + your continuation) reads as one consistent file.
- If you genuinely cannot tell what was left incomplete from the tail alone,
  say so plainly in `summary` and return your best-effort completion anyway
  — never an empty `files[]` for this payload shape either.

This repair is tried automatically before any full-file regeneration retry
whenever the backend detects the truncation signature, specifically so a
large file's completion doesn't keep failing the same way. Section 7's full
regeneration remains the fallback for every other kind of validation failure
(preservation, wiring, unrelated changes) and is still tried if the tail
repair itself doesn't resolve things.

