14. DIAGNOSTIC LOGGING (maintainers' note, not agent-facing)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The backend now emits one structured log line per pipeline stage — request
received, each validator's pass/fail with the exact error, which repair
strategy was chosen (tail-completion vs. full regeneration), each repair
call to the agent and what came back, and the final commit success or
exhausted-attempts failure. Every line is tagged `[TerrabotDiag]` and is a
single `event=... key=value ...` line, so the whole "backend asks agent /
agent responds / check fails / agent fixes it" story is readable directly
from the Function App Log Stream without needing to read source code. This
does not change any agent-facing behavior — it exists purely so failures can
be diagnosed (and fixes like Section 13 built) from what actually happened
in production, like this one was.

FOLLOW-UP FIX — the logs weren't showing up at all: the first version of
`_teams_diag_log` logged most events at INFO severity, and the logger's
effective level was never explicitly raised anywhere in the service. Under
the common default (root logger effectively at WARNING, which is typical for
an Azure Functions Python worker unless `host.json`/`logging.basicConfig`
says otherwise), every INFO-level call was silently dropped before it ever
reached the log stream — so the code was deployed and running (confirmed:
the exhaustion message the user saw used the new, updated error wording),
but its own diagnostics were invisible. Fixed by making `_teams_diag_log`
always emit at WARNING severity regardless of its `level` argument (kept
only as a readability tag inside the message text), plus a redundant stdout
`print()` as a second, independent channel, plus explicitly setting the
logger's own level. Verified against a simulated "root logger at WARNING,
no basicConfig" environment — both info- and warning-tagged diag calls now
surface. If diagnostics are STILL not visible after this fix, the next
thing to check is the Function App's own logLevel filtering in
`host.json` (the `logging.logLevel` section) — that operates a layer above
anything this service's own Python logging calls can control.

