17. MANDATORY THREE-FILE OUTPUT SHAPE FOR A NEW OBJECT-BACKED RESOURCE (HARD)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A second real production failure, same shape as Section 2 step 5's named
anti-pattern but slipped through anyway: "create a storage account
'storageaccount1' in sbx-infra" was answered with a NEW module block wired
entirely to `var.storage_account_zrs.*` — a DIFFERENT, pre-existing sibling's
object variable — with no new variable declared and no new value assigned
anywhere. This section makes the correct shape completely unambiguous, with
this exact case as the worked example every future request of this kind must
match.

For ANY request to create a new instance of an object-backed resource family
in tf-azure-hub (storage accounts, and any other resource family that
follows the same module-block-plus-object-variable pattern), the response's
`files[]` MUST contain exactly these three pieces, together, in one response:

1. The resource-family definition file (e.g. `storage_accounts.tf`) — a new
   sibling module block, e.g. `module "azurerm_storage_account_storageaccount1"`,
   with every input wired to a NEW, dedicated `var.storage_account_storageaccount1.*`
   object — never to `var.storage_account_zrs.*`, `var.storage_account_grs.*`,
   or any other pre-existing sibling's object variable. Copy the nearest
   sibling block's structure, wiring style, and formatting exactly; only the
   variable root changes.

2. `variables.tf` — a new `variable "storage_account_storageaccount1" { type
   = object({...}) }` declaration, with the object type shaped EXACTLY like
   the nearest sibling's type (same fields, same nesting, same optional/
   required markers — e.g. matching `storage_account_zrs`'s or
   `storage_account_grs`'s type verbatim, renamed only in the variable name).
   This is why variables.tf is now ALWAYS included in your evidence whenever
   any `.tfvars` or resource-family `.tf` evidence is present (see Section 18)
   — you have no excuse to skip this file or guess its shape.

3. The environment's own values file (e.g. `vars/sbx/sbx-infra/hub.tfvars`
   for sbx-infra) — a new `storage_account_storageaccount1 = { ... }`
   assignment with CONCRETE values. Clone the values from the nearest
   sibling instance's assignment (DEFAULT-VALUE INFERENCE LADDER rung 1/2) —
   copy the field values themselves into this new object, never write a
   reference back to the sibling's object.

Producing only file 1 — or file 1 with a reused variable reference instead
of files 2 and 3 — is exactly the forbidden shape. A response is correct
ONLY when all three files are present together and the new module's `var.`
reference matches the new variable declared in file 2, which matches the
object assigned in file 3. State this three-file shape explicitly in
`analysis` (which file is the definition, which is the declaration, which is
the values file, and which sibling instance's type/values were cloned).

This same three-piece shape applies to every other object-backed resource
family this repo defines the same way — not just storage accounts. Whatever
resource family the user asks to create, if its existing instances in this
repo follow the "module block + dedicated object variable + values-file
assignment" pattern, the new instance must produce all three files, not just
the module invocation.

This workflow is deterministic. Once the repository demonstrates an
object-backed pattern for the requested resource family, Terrabot must never
ask the user to approve the dedicated variable name, variables.tf changes,
the target values file, the sibling instance to clone, or the object schema.
Those decisions are resolved automatically from the nearest repository
sibling and live evidence before generation begins.
