28. TEAMS ENABLE/DISABLE FLAG-ONLY SELECTION — FINAL HARD OVERRIDE
================================================================================

This section applies only to Microsoft Teams requests whose original user action
is explicitly enable/disable/turn-on/turn-off/deactivate. It does not alter
resource creation, module selection, ordinary modification, PR/Jira, or branch
workflows.

1. The backend resolves the target environment first and inspects that
   environment's `main.tf`. For this explicit workflow the Terraform FILE is not
   a user choice. Never ask the user to choose backend.tf, outputs.tf,
   variables.tf, versions.tf, s3.tf, or any other file.

2. If one semantically matching existing Boolean flag is materially strongest,
   use it immediately. If multiple matching existing Boolean flags remain, ask
   exactly one question: which FLAG should be changed. Show each flag name plus
   concise context derived from the live repo (current bool value, enclosing
   module/block, and nearby same-family settings). The user replies by number or
   exact flag name.

3. Preserve original action polarity across every follow-up. A request that
   began as `disable patch setup in minidev` remains a DISABLE request even when
   the selected flag is named `enable_patch_monitoring`. The `enable_` prefix is
   part of the repository identifier; it must never reclassify the user's action.
   After selection, set the chosen currently-true flag to false. Conversely an
   original enable request sets the chosen currently-false flag to true.

4. A compact flag-name reply such as `enable_patch_monitoring` is a target
   selection, not a new freeform user request and not a new intent-classification
   turn. Continue the stored original request immediately; never ask "do you want
   me to enable..." merely because the selected identifier begins with `enable_`.

5. The same contract applies to AWS and Azure Teams enable/disable workflows.
   After the flag is selected, generate/materialize the complete final target
   `main.tf`, change only that Boolean assignment, preserve all unrelated code,
   run the existing semantic/HCL/fmt/preservation/self-correction checks, and
   push using the branch choice already resolved for the request.
